(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-hypertension-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Hypertension Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What are the characteristic symptoms of pheochromocytoma?","choices":["Snoring, apneas and daytime sleepiness","Flash pulmonary edema and an abdominal bruit","The five Ps: pressure, palpitations, pallor and tremor","Proximal weakness, bruising, thin skin and purple striae"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they are typically episodic and highly variable, which is why a normal examination between attacks does nothing to exclude the diagnosis in a patient who describes them. Perspiration is the fifth. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What proportion of adults have hypertension by current national survey data?","choices":["About one in ten","About one in four","Over three quarters","Close to half"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, at 47.7 percent using the threshold of 130/80 or current antihypertensive treatment. It is the single number that explains why this condition dominates primary care. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which retinal findings relate to CURRENT blood pressure only, rather than to current and previous?","choices":["Focal narrowing, hemorrhages and exudates","Generalized narrowing and arteriovenous nicking","Optic disc edema alone","Increased arteriolar light reflex alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, while generalized narrowing and nicking relate to both current and previous pressure. The distinction lets the fundus separate what is happening now from what has accumulated. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How does alcohol relate to blood pressure?","choices":["Excess raises it, and withdrawal can raise it acutely","Only chronic excess raises it; withdrawal has no effect","Moderate alcohol should be recommended for cardiovascular benefit","Alcohol lowers blood pressure and is protective"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and both directions matter clinically: the chronic intake contributes to the baseline, while withdrawal can produce a sudden severe elevation that looks like something else entirely. Withdrawal can produce acute severe hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the first-line imaging test to screen for renal artery stenosis?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography in every case","Plain abdominal radiography","Duplex ultrasound of the renal arteries","Catheter renal angiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because it screens blood flow without contrast or radiation. Computed tomography or magnetic resonance angiography give better anatomical detail and are chosen according to renal function and contrast tolerance. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why must an ACE inhibitor and an angiotensin receptor blocker never be combined?","choices":["The two cancel each other out pharmacologically","The combination is acceptable if potassium is monitored","Dual blockade raises hyperkalemia and kidney injury risk","The combination causes angioedema in most patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the phrasing is absolute rather than cautionary: one is used INSTEAD of the other, which matters because the two look complementary if the mechanism is read loosely. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which risk factors are listed for primary hypertension?","choices":["Unexplained hypokalemia with an adrenal mass","Loud snoring and witnessed apneas at night","Weak femoral pulses and an arm-to-leg pressure difference","Advancing age, family history, excess adiposity and high sodium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and most of them are modifiable, which is the basis for lifestyle treatment being recommended at every blood pressure category rather than only once medication starts. Physical inactivity, excess alcohol, dyslipidemia and insulin resistance complete the list. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What caution applies to thiazides in adults aged sixty-five and over?","choices":["Double the dose to overcome reduced renal clearance","Combine routinely with a beta blocker","Check sodium, particularly in women","Avoid them entirely in this age group"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside the volume status concern in older adults, which is why the monitoring after starting is more than a formality in this group. Those with low-normal sodium or existing hyponatremia need the same caution. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"How should electrocardiographic voltage criteria for left ventricular hypertrophy be interpreted?","choices":["They establish the ejection fraction","They are specific but not sensitive","They are sensitive but not specific","They reliably exclude hypertensive heart disease when normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Meeting the criteria means something, but failing to meet them means very little, which is why echocardiography is used when the clinical suspicion persists. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which pattern of onset should raise suspicion of a secondary cause?","choices":["Early onset, abrupt onset, resistance, or loss of stable control","Gradual elevation over many years in an older adult","Blood pressure that responds well to a single first-line agent","Elevated readings only in the clinic setting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All four are departures from the slow, age-related pattern of essential hypertension, and any of them is a reason to look for something driving it rather than simply adding another agent. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which combination must be avoided with verapamil or diltiazem?","choices":["A thiazide-type diuretic such as chlorthalidone","An angiotensin receptor blocker such as losartan","A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker such as amlodipine","A beta blocker, because of the risk of severe bradycardia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both slow the sinus and atrioventricular nodes, so combining them stacks the same effect, and both are also avoided where the ejection fraction is already reduced. Heart block is the other hazard. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which conditions are named as the common contributors to secondary hypertension?","choices":["Coarctation of the aorta and pheochromocytoma alone","Hyperthyroidism and Cushing syndrome alone","Excess dietary sodium and physical inactivity","Kidney disease, aldosteronism, sleep apnea and drugs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Knowing the short list is what makes the workup targeted: each has a first test attached to it, so the clues from the history point straight at which test to send. Renovascular disease belongs on that list too. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which symptoms in a hypertensive patient require immediate evaluation?","choices":["Confusion, focal deficit, abrupt severe headache or chest pain","Any headache at all in a patient with known hypertension","Mild ankle swelling on a calcium channel blocker","A dry cough after starting an ACE inhibitor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each maps to an organ under acute injury, which is what converts a high reading into an emergency and changes the timescale of management entirely. Visual disturbance, severe back pain and oliguria belong on the list too. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Among adults with hypertension, what does the control figure show?","choices":["Control figures are not measured in national surveys","Only about one in five reaches the target","Most patients reach the target once treated","Control and awareness are approximately equal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, at 20.7 percent achieving below 130/80. Awareness and treatment figures are both far higher, so the gap is between being treated and being controlled rather than between being ill and being diagnosed. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"In what order must blockade be established before surgery in pheochromocytoma, and why?","choices":["Neither is required before surgery","Alpha blockade first, then beta blockade","Beta blockade first, then alpha blockade","Either order, provided both are established before surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blocking beta receptors alone leaves alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed, so the pressure can rise sharply. The sequence is the safety point rather than the drug names. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why is blood pressure measured in both arms at the initial visit?","choices":["To average the two arms for the recorded value","To screen for coarctation of the aorta","Because one arm is always inaccurate","To find a difference and pick the arm for follow-up"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the higher consistent arm is used subsequently. An asymmetric reading is also one of the examination findings that raises concern for acute aortic pathology. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which study establishes the diagnosis of coarctation, and to whom is the patient referred?","choices":["Catheter angiography, with no referral needed","Echocardiography, with referral to a vascular surgeon","Renal duplex ultrasound, with referral to nephrology","Computed tomography alone, with referral to endocrinology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with computed tomography or magnetic resonance used to confirm. Transcatheter intervention is among the options, which is why the referral is to someone who can offer it. A structural cardiologist is the alternative referral. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Why does masked hypertension matter?","choices":["It only occurs in patients already on treatment","It carries real cardiovascular risk despite normal office readings","It is a benign pattern requiring no follow-up","It describes high office readings with normal home readings"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is the reason out-of-office measurement is not merely a way of avoiding overtreatment: it also finds the patients whose risk the clinic reading conceals. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the treatment goal for blood pressure?","choices":["Below 130/80, with lower values encouraged where appropriate","Below 140/90 for all adults","Below 120/80 for every patient without exception","Whatever value the patient tolerates without symptoms"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the same threshold defines the condition and measures control, which is what makes the national control figure of about one in five directly interpretable. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What does chronic pressure overload produce in the heart, and why does it matter?","choices":["Left ventricular hypertrophy with impaired diastolic relaxation","Dilation of the ventricle with reduced ejection fraction from the outset","Valvular calcification with progressive stenosis","Pericardial thickening and constriction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the word independently is the point: the hypertrophy is not merely a marker of how high the pressure has been, it carries risk of its own beyond the pressure that caused it. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"For which features should a patient with renovascular disease be referred early?","choices":["Recurrent flash pulmonary edema or progressive renal decline","Any degree of renal artery narrowing found on screening","A single elevated creatinine measurement","Fibromuscular dysplasia in an older man"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Medical management suits many atherosclerotic lesions, so these three are what mark out the patients in whom revascularization has the most to offer. Truly refractory control is the third trigger for early referral. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which thiazide-type diuretic is preferred, and what is monitored?","choices":["Spironolactone, with renal function monitoring","Chlorthalidone, with electrolytes and urate monitored","Hydrochlorothiazide, with no monitoring required","Furosemide, with potassium monitoring only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hyponatremia and hypokalemia are the electrolyte concerns, hyperuricemia may precipitate gout, and volume depletion matters especially in older adults. Volume status is watched as well, particularly in older adults. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is the initial biochemical test for pheochromocytoma?","choices":["The aldosterone to renin ratio","A low-dose dexamethasone suppression test","Thyroid-stimulating hormone","Plasma free metanephrines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Either has high sensitivity, which is what an initial test needs: the priority at this stage is not missing the diagnosis rather than avoiding a false positive. Twenty-four hour urinary fractionated metanephrines are the alternative. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What do most patients with mild to moderate primary hypertension report?","choices":["Palpitations and sweating in most cases","No reliable blood pressure-related symptoms","Headache that reliably tracks the blood pressure","Visual disturbance in most cases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and headache in particular is common but nonspecific, so it cannot be relied on to indicate the pressure. This is why the diagnosis rests on measurement rather than on how the patient feels. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Why should not all chronic kidney disease in a hypertensive patient be attributed to blood pressure?","choices":["Kidney disease cannot raise blood pressure","Albuminuria is not a marker of kidney damage","Other renal causes such as diabetes must be excluded","Hypertension never damages the kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Attributing everything to the pressure risks missing a treatable second disease, and the relationship is bidirectional anyway, so the presence of both does not establish which came first. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"When is echocardiography appropriate in hypertension?","choices":["Only once medication has failed","Only to measure blood pressure more accurately","Suspected heart failure or a significant murmur","In every newly diagnosed hypertensive patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is explicitly not required for every new hypertensive patient. A normal ejection fraction does not exclude preserved-fraction failure, so diastolic parameters and symptoms are read together. Hypertrophy on the electrocardiogram and unexplained dyspnea are the other indications. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the standing of beta blockers in uncomplicated hypertension?","choices":["The agent of choice in confirmed resistant hypertension","Not first-line, but preferred in heart failure or ischemic disease","First-line for all newly diagnosed hypertension regardless of comorbidity","Contraindicated in all hypertensive patients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The comorbidity rather than the pressure is what selects them, which is the general principle behind individualizing antihypertensive therapy. A recent myocardial infarction is the third preferred indication. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the underlying abnormality in primary aldosteronism?","choices":["Excess renin driving aldosterone production","Glucocorticoid excess promoting sodium retention","Catecholamine excess from an adrenal medullary tumor","Autonomous aldosterone production independent of renin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the autonomy is what the screening test detects: aldosterone is high while renin is suppressed, which is a combination that cannot occur when the system is behaving normally. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the caution regarding blood pressure management in acute stroke?","choices":["Acute stroke follows a distinct protocol of its own","The same targets apply as in chronic prevention","Blood pressure should be lowered as rapidly as possible in all strokes","Blood pressure should be left entirely untreated in acute stroke"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Driving the pressure to a chronic target in the acute setting can extend the injury, so the two situations are managed by different rules despite involving the same measurement. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does a radial-femoral delay suggest?","choices":["Acute aortic dissection","Renal artery stenosis","Coarctation of the aorta","Peripheral arterial disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it sits alongside the arm-to-leg pressure comparison as the bedside evidence for a structural obstruction between the two territories. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"}]};
